🐛 Describe the bug
The posix_fallocate() call added in #161910 (merged Oct 2, 2025) has incorrect error handling. Per POSIX, posix_fallocate() returns the error code directly as its return value and does not set errno. The current code ignores the return value and reads errno, which contains a stale value from a previous syscall. This makes the reported error misleading and the root cause impossible to diagnose.
Affected code
aten/src/ATen/MapAllocator.cpp, inside the HAVE_POSIX_FALLOCATE block:
#ifdef HAVE_POSIX_FALLOCATE
          if (flags_ & ALLOCATOR_MAPPED_SHAREDMEM) {
            for (;;) {
              if (posix_fallocate(fd, 0, static_cast<off_t>(size)) == 0) {
                break;
              }

              if (errno == EINTR) { // BUG: errno is not set by posix_fallocate — these checks read stale values
                continue;
              }

              if (errno == EINVAL || errno == EOPNOTSUPP) {
                // the underlying filesystem does not support the operation
                break;
              }

              TORCH_CHECK(false, "unable to allocate shared memory(shm) for file <", filename_, ">: ", c10::utils::str_error(errno), " (", errno, ")");
            }
          }
#endif
Suggested fix
Capture the return value of posix_fallocate():
#ifdef HAVE_POSIX_FALLOCATE
          if (flags_ & ALLOCATOR_MAPPED_SHAREDMEM) {
            for (;;) {
              int err = posix_fallocate(fd, 0, static_cast<off_t>(size));
              if (err == 0) {
                break;
              }

              if (err == EINTR) {
                continue;
              }

              if (err == EINVAL || err == EOPNOTSUPP) {
                // the underlying filesystem does not support the operation
                break;
              }

              TORCH_CHECK(false, "unable to allocate shared memory(shm) for file <",
                  filename_, ">: ", c10::utils::str_error(err), " (", err, ")");
            }
          }
#endif
Observed error
When using the default file_descriptor sharing strategy with a DataLoader (num_workers=30, prefetch_factor=2, large variable-resolution image tensors), the error manifests as:
Traceback (most recent call last):
  File ".../multiprocessing/queues.py", line 264, in _feed
    obj = _ForkingPickler.dumps(obj)
          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  File ".../multiprocessing/reduction.py", line 51, in dumps
    cls(buf, protocol).dump(obj)
  File ".../torch/multiprocessing/reductions.py", line 615, in reduce_storage
    fd, size = storage._share_fd_cpu_()
               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  File ".../torch/storage.py", line 524, in _share_fd_cpu_
    return super()._share_fd_cpu_(*args, **kwargs)
           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
RuntimeError: unable to allocate shared memory(shm) for file </torch_734336_1000688389_46>: Resource temporarily unavailable (11)

The reported errno 11 (EAGAIN / "Resource temporarily unavailable") is a stale value from a prior syscall. The actual error code returned by posix_fallocate() is discarded. At the time of the error, /dev/shm had 63 GB free (of 63 GB total), file descriptor limits were at 1M, and the system had 89 GB of available RAM — none of the standard resource constraints were hit.
Reference
From the POSIX specification for posix_fallocate:

Upon successful completion, posix_fallocate() shall return zero; otherwise, an error number shall be returned to indicate the error.

The function does not return -1 and set errno like most POSIX functions. It returns the error number directly.
Note
If this bug is considered valid, I'd like to give it a try at fixing it myself as I'd like to contribute to Pytorch.
Versions
PyTorch version: 2.10.0+cu128
Python version: 3.12.12 (Clang 21.1.4)
OS: Linux-6.8.0-1047-aws-x86_64-with-glibc2.35
cc @VitalyFedyunin @albanD @pragupta @ppwwyyxx

🐛 Describe the bug
Inductor C++ codegen on ARM/NEON generates code where a variable is used before it is declared, causing a compile error.
Generated code (condensed)
// tmp5 used here — NOT YET DECLARED
transpose_mxn<double, 3LL, 3LL, false>(
    in_ptr5 + (x0 + 3LL*x1 + 9LL*tmp5), 3LL, tmp9, 3LL);

for (long x0_inner = 0; x0_inner < 3LL; x0_inner++) {
    auto tmp0 = in_ptr0[0LL];
    auto tmp3 = int64_t(tmp0 + 17LL);
    auto tmp4 = tmp0 < 0;
    auto tmp5 = tmp4 ? tmp3 : tmp0;  // declared HERE, too late
}
The transpose_mxn call is hoisted above the loop that defines tmp5.
Error
error: use of undeclared identifier 'tmp5'

Compile flags include -D CPU_CAPABILITY_NEON (ARM vectorization path).
Environment

macOS 15.4 / Apple M3 Max (ARM64)
Python 3.12, torch built from source (main, commit 74b00562f5d)
Default inductor backend
